#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125274} :activity/id #uuid "55f1d2f4-1d80-580f-ae6f-7bce48469649" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050143 :topic/id #uuid "3055ed78-44c0-5be7-9e08-cb26d881941a" :topic/math-academy-id 1934} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125726 :tutorial/content "The **gradient** of a multivariable function is analogous to the derivative of a single-variable function.\n\nFor a function $f(x,y)$ whose partial derivatives exist at a point $(x,y),$ the **gradient vector** is denoted $\\nabla f(x,y)$ and can be found by computing the vector of partial derivatives:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f (x,y) &=\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\mathbf i + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b8c5c84-83d9-55aa-bb25-87cf1904ed2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2399 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "be8dc2ef-0d3b-53c7-a3c2-8d5efed1d5c4" :step/math-academy-id 10570 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125275}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125275 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124443 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124247 :question/id #uuid "526b792b-9952-5dae-bcd6-15564c0e013f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3131"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3991a54-342b-5cd0-87e2-606146240555" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573893}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Two Variables"} :step/id #uuid "bc0272c3-579a-51cf-8ff7-4a2addd6c160" :step/math-academy-id 10571 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125276}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125727 :tutorial/content "For a function of three variables $f(x,y,z)$, the gradient of $f$ is given by the vector  \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla \\! f (x,y,z) &= f_x(x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf i + f_y (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf j + f_z (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf k.\n                             \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFor a function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n)$ of $n$ variables, its gradient is usually given as a column vector:\n\n![](images/21/21e05d85506da576e293bce86b1ca98f4f5b101c49b0591a49d80d2a4b8bfcc1.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "be2e3466-803d-5c6c-aeb9-f6cc57b000f8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2400 :tutorial/title "The Gradient of a Function of More Than Two Variables"} :step/id #uuid "a7dd6ff5-617a-5a73-a336-1505e52faa0e" :step/math-academy-id 10574 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125277}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124444 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124248 :question/id #uuid "bfade25e-065a-5c66-a554-9ee4e9559a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-3132"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551602} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573869}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"} :step/id #uuid "d6afbd23-ca7a-5b4d-ba2e-965bf065da01" :step/math-academy-id 10575 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124445 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124249 :question/id #uuid "b2549d7b-07c2-5f2f-89a3-2cccecf2ba22" :question/math-academy-id "e-3133"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560256} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573938}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"} :step/id #uuid "ecb77e79-95bc-5d3f-bdaa-68737214a697" :step/math-academy-id 10578 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125728 :tutorial/content "Since the gradient of a function of several variables is made up of partial derivatives, we can expect it to have properties that are similar to partial derivatives.\n\nAssume that $f$ and $g$ are functions whose first-order partial derivatives exist and are continuous on some open domain, and that $a,b,$ and $c$ are real constants. Then, the gradient has the following properties:\n\n- Constant rule:  $\\nabla c = \\mathbf 0$\n\n- Linearity: $\\nabla (af + bg) = a \\nabla\\! f + b\\nabla\\! g$\n\n- Product Rule: $\\nabla(fg) = g \\nabla\\! f + f \\nabla\\! g$\n\n- Quotient Rule: $\\nabla \\left( \\dfrac{f}{g}\\right) = \\dfrac{ g \\nabla\\! f - f \\nabla\\! g}{g^2}$" :tutorial/id #uuid "52036b8f-4d6f-5609-bbcf-fc34a51d9b98" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2401 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Gradient"} :step/id #uuid "d4e8b345-fc40-5dfb-ab00-d4b25041d02c" :step/math-academy-id 10581 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125281}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124447 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124251 :question/id #uuid "67e9bcb5-4bf2-56b8-8d82-95355841eaaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3150"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524196} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573946}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"} :step/id #uuid "acd8b544-63b3-5271-9729-c95afd0bee1d" :step/math-academy-id 10701 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125462}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125462 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125806 :tutorial/content "For a multivariable function, the existence of the gradient implies continuity.\n\nA sufficient condition for the gradient ![](images/3a/3a172b4fd0180b172579ebac50f73d8dfb75a219a02b610f666d1a4e446592a2.svg) to exist is that the partial derivatives ![](images/f2/f20807159d7e3d172a1ed9d92b8001a4c186e78ef3b8423592c75e44b633ec88.svg) and ![](images/f4/f423cce200533b35d0f6df2d4a6f5e917529276e749a7ab4995930d64559c98d.svg) both exist in a neighborhood of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and are continuous at ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg).\n\nTherefore, if ![](images/f2/f20807159d7e3d172a1ed9d92b8001a4c186e78ef3b8423592c75e44b633ec88.svg) and ![](images/f4/f423cce200533b35d0f6df2d4a6f5e917529276e749a7ab4995930d64559c98d.svg) both exist in a neighborhood of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and are continuous at ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg), then ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) is continuous at ![](images/85/853e57b0bf9a8f7c16e59d37575ae23ac4028ed66b30720af9d7d49ab595c91b.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** The continuity of the partial derivatives is crucial. The existence of the partial derivatives alone does not imply that a function is continuous at a point." :tutorial/id #uuid "652f49b9-1a78-58d1-9531-1e115634f86a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3864 :tutorial/title "Continuous Partial Derivatives Imply Continuity"} :step/id #uuid "900dafcb-5560-5f91-8fbe-4fbd70dce61e" :step/math-academy-id 22631}] :activity/title "The Gradient Vector" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124727}]}
